WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.799
Peter Desberg, Author: So when do the interesting
questions start?

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:07.299
Film Courage: Right now, actually.

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:13.210
But this [question] is a little bit leading…why
are so many screenwriters opposed to structure?

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.520
Jeffrey Davis, Author: I didn’t know they
were?

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:26.210
My experience is that there are two groups
of screenwriters now.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:31.320
There used to be…I mean you probably remember
in the 90’s and early 2000’s, there was

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.969
the Chris Vogler book (which is pretty good
actually) and the [John] Truby book and who

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:40.449
is the guy who charges almost as much as the
EST seminars?

00:00:40.449 --> 00:00:44.809
And it used to be like this is how you write
a screenplay.

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:53.539
And I think that particularly in TV screenplays
and teleplays, that’s changing.

00:00:53.539 --> 00:01:03.180
I would like it…again to me…structure
is the easiest thing to learn that the craft

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.570
of it…that’s not even to me the craft
of it.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:11.460
I could basically teach anybody how to structure
a screenplay.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.780
And I’m lucky to have Peter because (I’m
not joking around) he’s really good at structure.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:24.100
But I believe structure comes out of character
and conflict, so I have not against a lot

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.520
of writers who are opposed to that.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:34.380
I just think what some teachers do is they
will teach structure before they teach character

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.840
or conflict which is a big mistake because
I’ve read things that are beautifully structured

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:41.840
and empty.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.670
Peter Desberg: There is software now that
you can get to structure your screenplay.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.160
Mariner makes a whole bunch of software products.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.570
Jeffrey Davis: That’s easy.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.670
I could teach you all of that, formatting
and structuring and all that.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.620
But I hadn’t heard what you’re hearing.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.480
Are you hearing that a lot of people are opposed
to structure?

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.800
Film Courage: It was a question to see what
your answer would be.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.800
Jeffrey Davis: Well for God’s sake Karen.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:01.800
I’m so disappointed.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.800
Film Courage: Maybe it’s a rumor.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.710
But I’m just throwing it out there.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:07.930
Jeffrey Davis: It’s funny because I hear
the opposite.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:11.670
I hear people fall back on structure.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.890
Not so much TV, I guess?

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:17.530
Peter Desberg: There are a bunch of really
funny videos on Youtube of fake pitches.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.739
They are hysterical.

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.650
There is a wonderful on…

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.380
Jeffrey Davis: …On pitching CITIZEN KANE.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.380
It’s hysterical.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.730
GONE WITH THE WIND…

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:37.849
Peter Desberg: I’m trying to think about
the one I really like here…PULP FICTION?

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:41.409
“These pages are all out of order.

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:42.590
So don’t worry.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.560
We made the story flow in a logical sequence
now.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:54.540
So again (I think like many other areas) the
concept of structure is evolving also.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.810
But that started a long time ago.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.769
That probably started in the 60’s with the
new French Novel and stuff where all of the

00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:04.269
sudden you don’t know if you’re seeing
a sequence, whether you are seeing a composite

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:08.980
or which part is fantasy in someone’s head
and which part is real.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.790
So the whole notion of structure is evolving
a lot.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:16.209
So things aren’t necessarily as linear as
they were.

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:20.541
People are taking bigger liberties that way
(which is a good thing).

00:03:20.541 --> 00:03:25.909
Jeffrey Davis: I think you have to write the
way that…now I’m not talking about being

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:29.310
in a [TV writer’s] room…then you’ve
got to observe the rules that the Showrunner

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.810
lays out.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:35.799
But if you’re writing something of your
own and if for example I always tell students

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:41.209
and I tell myself that if you are awakened
at 3 o’clock in the morning and a scene

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:44.279
comes to you, get up and write it down.

00:03:44.279 --> 00:03:47.579
And don’t worry about where it goes in the
structure.

00:03:47.579 --> 00:03:51.110
But if you wait, you might still be able to
write the scene but it won’t have the same

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.530
power and the passion that you felt.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.739
It woke you up for a reason.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:59.680
And a lot of writer’s (mainly students)
are afraid to do that because they’ve had

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.559
pounded into your head that you must write
everything in order and that’s the part

00:04:06.559 --> 00:04:07.559
that makes me crazy.

00:04:07.559 --> 00:04:08.959
That’s the part that makes me crazy.

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:09.959
How can you write everything in order?

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:12.439
There’s a famous quote…I forget who said
it?

00:04:12.439 --> 00:04:14.510
You know, I am blanking on everything today.

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:15.719
But I do know Franchise now.

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:19.090
I am not going to forget that.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.240
“How do I know what I think until I see
what I say?”

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.560
Film Courage: Oh wow!

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.560
That’s deep.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:25.560
Jeffrey Davis: Isn’t that powerful?

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.680
Film Courage: Is this what Adam has hanging
over his desk?

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.970
[Referring to this video on Pitching a TV
Series]

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.310
Jeffrey Davis: Yes…see you’ve got a new
quality for him.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:38.610
I mean, I think a lot of people are afraid
to dive into…I am…to just dive into that

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:43.229
darkness and let it be bad and find the structure.

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:47.060
But make sure that you find it from the characters
because otherwise it will be empty.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.280
Film Courage: Do you find certain students
and some of the people you’ve worked with

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.250
are okay with going to those [dark] places
and others are really (whether it’s their

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:56.250
upbringing) they’re scared?

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.960
Jeffrey Davis: Nobody is okay with it.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.190
You get them to be okay with it.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.389
And what I’m doing, I’m teaching.

00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:08.100
I co-created a course a couple years ago (a
remade course for freshman).

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.629
And it was the first day of class (it will
be a week tomorrow).

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:17.560
And I said to everybody in the room “Get
ready to embrace sh*t!”

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.740
Uh oh…are you going to have to erase that?

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:20.740
Film Courage: No…

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.960
Jeffrey Davis: So it’s the Anne Lamott dictum
from [her bestselling book] Bird By Bird.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.930
You have an obligation to be sh**ty.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:36.840
Which you can almost see the weight come off
their shoulders.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.289
It’s the closest I’ve feel to being like
a therapist.

00:05:40.289 --> 00:05:46.289
I’m releasing them from the need to be good.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:47.770
Because you’re going to be anyway!

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.449
You’re 18-years-old!

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:51.460
How good are you going to be!

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:54.300
And I don’t give you a good grade for being
good.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.909
And then they go “Wait a minute?

00:05:55.909 --> 00:05:59.250
If we try to be good you’re not going to
grade us better than people who try to be

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:00.550
bad?”

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.849
Exactly!

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:04.729
Because I don’t care, I’m interested in
the process, not the product.

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:05.840
You’re 18.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.120
It doesn’t matter how good it is, it’s
still going to s*ck.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.180
Film Courage: That word we can’t use.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.360
Jeffrey Davis: I’m sorry.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:15.990
Peter Desberg: 18?

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:17.629
Film Courage: Yes!

00:06:17.629 --> 00:06:23.691
Jeffrey Davis: This is a very big thing with
me, this need people have…and I think this

00:06:23.691 --> 00:06:30.910
is because I’m a recovering perfectionist…and
it’s another thing this partnership has

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:32.050
given me.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:37.069
He forces me to put it down.

00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:42.040
And if I had my druthers (not so much now,
but it used to be) I would just take forever

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.919
because I didn’t want to be bad.

00:06:43.919 --> 00:06:46.699
Peter Desberg: Yeah…the P word is a synonym
for scared.

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:48.110
Film Courage: Oh…right…right.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:53.480
Jeffrey Davis: I’m afraid it will s*ck and
if you tell students at the beginning “It’s

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:54.480
okay to s*ck.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.560
It’s okay to be bad.”

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:03.260
It’s like Peter said earlier, than you have
something that’s on paper you can play with

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:04.260
it forever.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.819
But if you don’t put it down, you’ve got
nothing.

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:09.819
Peter Desberg: We were talking about a guy
this morning who has been working for 5 years

00:07:09.819 --> 00:07:10.880
on a screenplay.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:11.949
Jeffrey Davis: 5?

00:07:11.949 --> 00:07:12.949
10 [years].

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:14.079
5 I could accept.

00:07:14.079 --> 00:07:18.539
You know it’s not like he’s writing the
great American novel, he’s writing a screenplay.

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:19.539
C’mon?

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:20.539
Really?

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:23.419
Film Courage: Well, unless he’s so lost
in the process…

00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:26.800
Jeffrey Davis: Then he should be writing 3
screenplays, not 1.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.669
Peter Desberg: Double-spaced, 12 pages a year.

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:31.370
I think he needs a little more output than
that.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:37.069
Jeffrey Davis: I think that it is just fear
and older people have fear, too.

00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:38.629
Film Courage: Sure…yeah.

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:40.909
I think it’s okay to be young and to fail.

00:07:40.909 --> 00:07:42.280
But older people, you have to…

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:47.289
Jeffrey Davis: One of the worst days of my
life is the day when I finish a play or I

00:07:47.289 --> 00:07:52.460
finish something and my first reader is my
wife because she’s an actress.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:56.069
She’ll say nice things and then she’ll
say “So basically what you’re asking me

00:07:56.069 --> 00:08:00.449
to do is put marbles in my mouth to say these
lines.”

00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:04.789
And after I get over being hurt and thinking
about divorce court and all of those things

00:08:04.789 --> 00:08:08.419
and I realize, you know she’s right?

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:10.639
Again, we are writing for actors.

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:15.659
We’re not writing…yes producers have to
read it.

00:08:15.659 --> 00:08:21.389
Executives have to read it but you’re going
to be replaced really fast if you can’t

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:22.039
write dialogue.

